Prompt 5: Training / Inference Ideation Prompt ( K = K{=} num_ideas ) You are a research ideation assistant. Given a set of source documents, your job is to generate {num_ideas} distinct research ideas that could inspire a scientist working in or adjacent to the area covered by these documents. Each idea is a single card in the schema below. The four fields play DIFFERENT roles; a card whose fields collapse into restatements of each other is not useful. Two of the fields must begin with a bracketed [label] chosen from the taxonomy that follows the schema. SCHEMA (one card per idea) • problem_gap: The gap the idea addresses, drawn from across the source documents -- an open question, tension, or unresolved difficulty that connects them and that none resolves on its own. State it as one clear gap, NOT as your specific solution to it. BEGIN this field with the single best-fitting OPPORTUNITY PATTERN label in square brackets (from the taxonomy below), then the prose gap (1--2 sentences). Pick the ONE dominant pattern even if several apply; if NONE genuinely fits, use ‘‘[Other]’’ rather than forcing a poor match. Example: ‘‘[Bridge Opportunity] Work on X and on Y each … but neither connects …’’. • core_claim: The couple of sentences the whole idea lives or dies on -- ‘‘we claim that X.’’ It must be something that could turn out to be wrong: a definite, testable assertion / hypothesis / insight, not a topic or a direction. Do not just restate the gap, and do not describe what you would do about it. From these sentences alone a reader should know exactly what the idea is betting on (1--2 sentences). • method: At a high level, HOW the claim would be turned into research -- the kind of move (combining ideas, extending to a new setting, making a fragile result robust, deriving formally, mapping a space empirically, building an artifact) and how it addresses the gap. It should be a PROPOSED approach, forward-looking: use present/infinitive framing (‘‘to test this, X’’; ‘‘the approach is to X’’). Keep it at the level of an approach, NOT a step-by-step protocol. BEGIN this field with the single best-fitting METHOD PARADIGM label in square brackets (from the taxonomy below), then the prose approach (2--3 sentences). Pick the ONE dominant paradigm even if several apply; if NONE genuinely fits, use ‘‘[Other]’’. Example: ‘‘[Synthesis / Unification] To test this, the approach is to …’’. • inspiration: Prose (three to five sentences) that ARGUES how the idea arises FROM the source documents -- the relationship BETWEEN them, not a per-document restatement. This field must be CRITICAL, not a credit ledger. Two things it must do: (1) NAME EACH CITED SOURCE’S LIMITATION -- for every Document you lean on, say what it got wrong, missed, stopped short of, or left unresolved, so the idea reads as a RESPONSE to a concrete shortcoming (‘‘Document 3 measured X but only under assumption A’’; ‘‘Document 5 proposed Y yet never addressed Z’’); and (2) JUSTIFY THE NON-OBVIOUS LEAP -- argue WHY connecting these documents is non-trivial: the tension, friction, or mismatch that had to be resolved for the pieces to fit, so the idea does not read as the obvious next step. If the connection IS routine, say so plainly rather than inflating it. Refer to the numbered documents by their exact ‘‘Document ⟨ \langle N ⟩ \rangle ’’ labels wherever the idea genuinely builds on them. If you draw on any idea or analogy from OUTSIDE the provided documents, name that external source explicitly (it carries no Document ⟨ \langle N ⟩ \rangle ). Do not invent a document link. Write problem_gap, core_claim, and method so they: state the gap, the claim/bet, and the approach, NOT the outcome. Keep problem_gap broad enough that the core_claim is doing real narrowing work. TAXONOMY (for the required leading [labels]) problem_gap MUST begin with exactly ONE of these OPPORTUNITY PATTERNS, in square brackets and written verbatim: • [Puzzle / Contradiction] -- a paradox, tradeoff, surprising failure, or conflicting evidence. • [Explanation Gap] -- a missing causal, mechanistic, theoretical, or explanatory account. • [Scope Mismatch] -- unrealistic assumptions, narrow regimes, unclear transferability, or boundary conditions. • [Evidence Gap] -- missing ways to observe, measure, benchmark, audit, diagnose, validate, compare, or accumulate evidence. • [Bridge Opportunity] -- disconnected literatures, theories, evidence streams, communities, or methods that could be connected. • [Failure / Risk Gap] -- brittleness, unreliability, bias, uncertainty, safety/privacy/security risk, or reproducibility concerns. • [Resource Bottleneck] -- cost, compute, time, data access, sample scarcity, experimental burden, deployment friction, usability, or scalability. method MUST begin with exactly ONE of these METHOD PARADIGMS, in square brackets and written verbatim: • [Synthesis / Unification] -- bridges, integrates, reconciles, or unifies separate literatures, theories, evidence streams, mechanisms, or methods. • [Relax / Extend Scope] -- makes prior work function under weaker assumptions, broader scope, new regimes, noisier conditions, or realistic settings. • [Robustification] -- reduces failures, brittleness, risk, uncertainty, bias, unreliability, or trustworthiness problems. • [Formal Derivation] -- introduces a formal model, theorem, bound, objective, derivation, proof, ontology, taxonomy, conceptual distinction, or explanatory formulation. • [Empirical Mapping] -- builds or applies systematic measurement, benchmarks, diagnostics, datasets, empirical maps, comparative studies, or pattern analyses. • [Artifact / System] -- builds a concrete artifact, software system, platform, device, material, prototype, or deployment workflow as the central contribution. • [Optimization / Search] -- uses optimization, search, screening, tuning, active/adaptive design, scaling, resource allocation, or efficiency strategies to discover or improve a solution. If, and only if, none of the seven listed labels on an axis genuinely fits, you may use ‘‘[Other]’’ for that axis -- do not stretch a listed label to force a fit, and do not invent any label outside this list plus ‘‘[Other]’’. Use the bracketed label ONLY at the very start of the field; do not repeat it in the prose or add labels to core_claim or inspiration. DIVERSITY BAR (across the {num_ideas} ideas) The {num_ideas} ideas together should span: • Multiple distinct problem_gaps (not variants of the same question). • A mix of opportunity patterns and method paradigms -- do not produce ideas that all carry the same two [labels]. • Different core_claims -- even when two ideas share a gap, they must offer materially different bets on it. Avoid: paraphrasing the source documents, restating consensus findings as ‘‘claims,’’ and cards whose core_claim collapses into the problem_gap. ORDERING Put your strongest single idea first -- most impactful, best-grounded, most defensible. Each subsequent idea should be as distinct as possible from the ones already listed, but equally impactful (if possible). OUTPUT FORMAT Each idea object must have exactly this structure: {
  "problem_gap": "[<one opportunity pattern>] ...",
  "core_claim": "...",
  "method": "[<one method paradigm>] ...",
  "inspiration": "... Document <N> did X but stopped short of ...;
    Document <M> offered the method yet ...; connecting them is
    non-obvious because ... (name any external source explicitly) ..."
} SOURCE DOCUMENTS {documents} TASK Generate {num_ideas} research ideas conforming to the schema, taxonomy, diversity bar, and ordering rule above. Think before writing -- sketch the space of possible gaps and claims internally, then reason through them, eliminating ideas that either have already been implemented, or do not make any logical sense. Then commit to {num_ideas} that together cover that space well. Structure your response in two parts: 1. <think> ... </think>: Use this block to sketch the space of possible gaps and claims before committing. Inside, work through: what are the main threads or open questions running through the documents; what candidate problem_gaps could be posed, and which are broad enough to admit multiple distinct core_claims; for each promising gap, what core_claim -- drawn from the sources or a related domain -- gives it a distinctive angle, and what method paradigm would carry it (note the two [labels]); and which {num_ideas} cards together cover the space well and hit the diversity bar. This block is for your reasoning only and will not be parsed as output. 2. <answer> ... </answer>: Place the final JSON list of {num_ideas} idea objects inside this block, and nothing else. The content of <answer> must be a valid JSON list parseable on its own -- no prose, no markdown code fences, no commentary before or after the list, no enclosing object around the list. Do not produce any text outside of these two blocks.

Prompt 6: Impact / Quality Judge Prompt (SciJudge, pairwise) System: You are a helpful assistant. You first think about the reasoning process in your mind and then provide the user with the answer. User: Today is {today}. Below are two research ideas, A and B. Determine which idea is likely to have higher scientific impact (i.e. would attract more follow-up work and citations). Show your reasoning process in <reason> </reason> tags. And return the final answer in <answer> </answer> tags. The final answer should contain only ‘A’ or ‘B’. Idea A:
{abstract_a} Idea B:
{abstract_b}

